Over the last thirty years different plastic analyses of steel structures have emerged. Basically, they can be divided into two groups:
- Model of localized plasticity – plastic hinges;
- Model of plastic zones- this study is used in present paper.
The method of plastic zones is an accurate method of elastic-plastic analysis of frame structures, which takes into consideration extension both as in cross sections as in along elements. Also it takes into consideration the change of position of neutral axis, with a concern on the size of elastic segment (progress of plastic areas) and different possibilities of dis- attribution of residual stresses resulting in end effects of large deflection. The main characteristic of this model are:
- Possibility of gradual plasticity of cross sections – gradual decrease of stiffness at cross section;
- Possibility of extension of plastic zones in definite zones - decrease of stiffness at the system level;
- Following redistribution of forces at a cross section at any level of load;
• Involving consideration of an initial imperfection and backward tensions.
The model developed in present paper was based on the finite element method for elastic- plastic materials. This numerical analysis involves the M-C elastic- plastic principle. The description of the problem is a fully three-dimensional (3-D) representation of the wellbore and the applied stresses. For simplicity, A 2-D model was also developed, followed by similar representation of 3-D. Both models of virtual boreholes are presented in appendix A. It is a cubic block of hard rock (shale, treated as steel) with a borehole drilled through its center. For model simplicity, four nodes, plane strain quadrilateral bilinear solid continuum infinite elements are used. To avoid the boundary effects, this model is designed by using of infinite element segment at the extension of the model. Due to symmetry consideration, only a quarter of the structure (90 degrees on the horizontal plane) was modeled for 2-D analysis. An 8.5 inch (22 cm) hole was modeled with plane strain conditions. The schematic structure used for this study is shown in Fig.12 (left, 2-D) and boundary condition for horizontal plane is shown in Fig.12 (right). This model has not employed in gravity and chemical instability effect. For case sensitivity analysis, only vertical wellbore is chosen to be analysed. However, this model can be used for inclined wells. The simulation wellbores can be rotated at 0, 15, 30, 45, 60, 75 and 90 degrees from the vertical axis, in a plane perpendicular to $\sigma_{\mathrm{H}}$ to quantify the borehole inclination effect on the materials state. The differential stress is maximum on this particular plane ( $\sigma_{1}-\sigma_{3}$ ) and probably it represents the worse case. Since, several real-life situations were evaluated. The potential applicability of the results obtained here is apparent; adaption of such technique in drilling in hard rock is also apparent; and the results of these simulations could be used as a cross check for made more particular situation in the field